3.2 Concepts

The toolchain
Programming a microcontroller involves a cross-compiler (it runs on your laptop but produces code for the ESP32), libraries for the chip’s hardware, and an uploader that writes the compiled program into the board’s flash memory.
The two most common environments are the Arduino IDE and PlatformIO. This book uses PlatformIO, an extension for VS Code, because it:

supports several frameworks for the same chip (Arduino and Espressif’s own ESP-IDF);
manages library dependencies per project, with version numbers, in a single platformio.ini file;
integrates building, uploading, the Serial Monitor and debugging into one editor.

A minimal kit project looks like this:
; platformio.ini
[env:esp32dev]
platform = espressif32@^6.9.0      ; pins the Arduino-ESP32 core (2.x series)
board = esp32dev
framework = arduino
monitor_speed = 115200
lib_deps =
    adafruit/Adafruit SSD1306@^2.5.13
Pinning the platform version matters: different versions of the Arduino-ESP32 core have different APIs for some peripherals, and an unpinned project can stop compiling when a new version is released. Setup instructions are in Appendix B.


The Arduino programming model
An Arduino program (a sketch) defines two functions:

setup() runs once after power-up or reset. Configure pins, start serial ports and initialize devices here.
loop() runs repeatedly, forever, until the board loses power.

#include &lt;Arduino.h&gt;
#include "iot_pins.h"            // IoTPins::LED_1 (red), LED_2 (yellow), LED_3 (green), BUTTON_1, ...

void setup() {
  pinMode(IoTPins::LED_1, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IoTPins::BUTTON_1, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  bool pressed = (digitalRead(IoTPins::BUTTON_1) == LOW);   // active low, see below
  digitalWrite(IoTPins::LED_1, pressed ? HIGH : LOW);
}
Behind the scenes the Arduino framework provides main(), calls setup() once, and then calls loop() in an endless loop. On the ESP32, that loop actually runs as a task under a real-time operating system (Chapter 4), but for now you can think of it as a simple infinite loop.
Arduino code is C++. Most sketches use a C-like subset, but classes, references and the standard library are all available. Because int is 16 bits on some boards and 32 bits on others, prefer the fixed-width types uint8_t, int16_t, uint32_t and so on whenever size matters.


Digital input and output
A digital signal has two levels. On the ESP32, a pin reads HIGH (1) near 3.3 V and LOW (0) near 0 V.







Function
Purpose




pinMode(pin, mode)
Configure a pin as OUTPUT, INPUT, INPUT_PULLUP or INPUT_PULLDOWN


digitalWrite(pin, value)
Drive an output pin HIGH or LOW


digitalRead(pin)
Read an input pin as HIGH or LOW





Pull-up and pull-down resistors
A button connected between a pin and ground only defines the pin’s level while it is pressed. When it is released, the pin is connected to nothing. It is floating, and it will pick up electrical noise and read random values.
A pull-up resistor connects the pin weakly to 3.3 V, so it reads HIGH until the button pulls it firmly to ground. A pull-down resistor does the opposite. The ESP32 has internal pull-up and pull-down resistors (roughly 45 kΩ) on most pins, enabled with INPUT_PULLUP or INPUT_PULLDOWN.
The kit’s buttons are wired from GPIO 32 and 33 to ground and use INPUT_PULLUP. They are therefore active low: digitalRead() returns LOW when the button is pressed. Forgetting this inverts every button test.
Remember that GPIO 34–39 have no internal pull resistors. A button on one of those pins needs an external resistor.


Debouncing
Mechanical switch contacts bounce when they close or open. For a few milliseconds the pin rapidly flips between HIGH and LOW before settling. A microcontroller samples fast enough to see every bounce, so a single press can be counted many times.
Debouncing filters out the bounces. The simplest software approach accepts a new state only once it has been stable for a short time, such as 20–50 ms:
constexpr uint32_t DEBOUNCE_MS = 30;

bool readButtonDebounced(uint8_t pin) {
  static bool stableState = HIGH;     // last accepted state (released)
  static bool lastReading = HIGH;
  static uint32_t lastChange = 0;

  bool reading = digitalRead(pin);
  if (reading != lastReading) {       // something changed: restart the timer
    lastChange = millis();
    lastReading = reading;
  }
  if (millis() - lastChange &gt;= DEBOUNCE_MS) {
    stableState = reading;            // stable long enough: accept it
  }
  return stableState;
}
Detecting double clicks, long presses and so on builds on the same idea. Well-tested libraries such as Button2 handle debouncing and click patterns for you, and are usually the right choice in a project.


Polling and interrupts
The loop above polls the button: it checks the pin on every pass, even though the answer is almost always “no change”. Polling is simple and adequate when the loop is fast and the event is slow.
An interrupt lets the hardware notify the processor when a pin changes. The processor pauses whatever it was doing, runs a short function called an interrupt service routine (ISR), and then resumes. Every ESP32 GPIO pin can trigger an interrupt.
volatile uint32_t pressCount = 0;     // shared with the ISR: must be volatile

void IRAM_ATTR onButton() {           // keep the ISR in fast internal RAM
  pressCount++;                       // do the minimum, then return
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(IoTPins::BUTTON_1, INPUT_PULLUP);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(IoTPins::BUTTON_1), onButton, FALLING);
}

void loop() {
  static uint32_t lastReported = 0;
  uint32_t count = pressCount;        // copy once, then work with the copy
  if (count != lastReported) {
    Serial.printf("Presses: %lu\n", count);
    lastReported = count;
  }
}
The trigger mode says which change causes the interrupt: RISING, FALLING, CHANGE, LOW or HIGH. Because the button is active low, a press is a FALLING edge.
ISRs come with strict rules:

Keep them short. Set a flag, increment a counter or record a timestamp, and return. Do the real work in loop().
Do not block or print. No delay(), no Serial.print(), no I²C or SPI transactions.
Mark shared variables volatile, so the compiler always reads them from memory instead of a stale copy in a register.
Place the ISR in IRAM with IRAM_ATTR on the ESP32, so it can run even while the flash memory is busy.

Interrupts do not remove bouncing. The ISR above will count bounces as presses. A common fix is to record the time in the ISR and ignore edges that arrive within the debounce window of the previous one.


Analog input
The physical world is mostly analog: temperature, light level and the position of a knob vary continuously. A computer can only store numbers, so an analog-to-digital converter (ADC) measures a voltage and quantizes it into one of a fixed number of steps.
The number of steps is set by the converter’s resolution. An \(n\)-bit ADC divides its input range into \(2^n\) levels. The ESP32’s ADCs are 12-bit by default, so analogRead() returns a value from 0 to 4095 over roughly 0 to 3.3 V, and each step is about 0.8 mV.
int raw = analogRead(IoTPins::POT_ADC);                  // 0..4095
float volts = raw * 3.3f / 4095.0f;                       // approximate
int percent = map(raw, 0, 4095, 0, 100);                  // rescale
The ESP32 uses successive-approximation ADCs, which find the value bit by bit, like a binary search. Keep three practical limits in mind:

Only the ADC1 pins (GPIO 32–39) work while Wi-Fi is on.
The ESP32’s ADC is not perfectly linear, especially near 0 V and near the top of the range. For accurate measurements, use the calibrated analogReadMilliVolts() function, or an external ADC.
Readings are noisy. Averaging several samples is cheap and effective.



Analog output: DAC and PWM
There are two ways to produce an output between “fully on” and “fully off”.
A digital-to-analog converter (DAC) produces a real intermediate voltage. The original ESP32 has two 8-bit DAC channels, on GPIO 25 and 26: dacWrite(25, 128) outputs about half of 3.3 V.
Pulse-width modulation (PWM) is far more common. The pin switches rapidly between HIGH and LOW, and the fraction of time it spends HIGH, the duty cycle, sets the average power. An LED switched fast enough appears dimmer; a motor runs slower. A servo reads the width of each pulse as a position command (Chapter 7).
The ESP32 generates PWM with its LEDC peripheral, which has 16 channels with configurable frequency and resolution:
// Arduino-ESP32 core 2.x API (the kit's pinned version)
constexpr int LED_CHANNEL = 1, PWM_FREQ = 5000, PWM_BITS = 8;

void setup() {
  ledcSetup(LED_CHANNEL, PWM_FREQ, PWM_BITS);
  ledcAttachPin(IoTPins::LED_3, LED_CHANNEL);
}

void loop() {
  for (int duty = 0; duty &lt;= 255; duty += 5) {   // fade in
    ledcWrite(LED_CHANNEL, duty);
    delay(20);
  }
}
Version 3.x of the Arduino-ESP32 core replaced ledcSetup() and ledcAttachPin() with a single ledcAttach(pin, freq, bits) and lets ledcWrite() take the pin directly. This is one reason the kit pins its platform version. Also note that tone() and the servo library both use LEDC channels; the kit’s starter code assigns them separate channels so they do not interfere.


Time without blocking
delay(ms) stops the program completely. While it waits, the loop cannot read buttons, refresh the display or answer network messages. A better pattern is to check how much time has passed and act only when it is due:
void loop() {
  static uint32_t lastRed = 0, lastYellow = 0;
  uint32_t now = millis();

  if (now - lastRed &gt;= 1000) {           // every 1 s
    lastRed = now;
    digitalWrite(IoTPins::LED_1, !digitalRead(IoTPins::LED_1));
  }
  if (now - lastYellow &gt;= 1500) {        // every 1.5 s
    lastYellow = now;
    digitalWrite(IoTPins::LED_2, !digitalRead(IoTPins::LED_2));
  }
  // ... the loop stays free to read inputs here
}
millis() returns the milliseconds since start-up as a 32-bit unsigned number, which wraps around to zero after about 49.7 days. Writing the comparison as now - last &gt;= interval, with unsigned arithmetic, gives the correct answer even across the wraparound. Writing it as now &gt;= last + interval does not.






TipTry it on your kit: buttons and interrupts



This exercise corresponds to the second lecture activity and runs in Wokwi or on Stage 1 of the kit.

Count button presses by polling without any debouncing, and print the count. Press the button 20 times. What count do you get?
Add the debounce function and repeat. What changed?
Rewrite the counter with an interrupt. Does it count bounces? Fix it by ignoring edges that arrive within 30 ms of the previous one.
Make the red LED blink every 500 ms and the green LED every 700 ms using millis(), while the button still toggles the yellow LED instantly.
Read the potentiometer and use it to set the green LED’s brightness with PWM.

A guided, step-by-step version for a lecture session is Activity 2.

4.2 Concepts

What makes a system “embedded”
There is no precise definition of an embedded system, but embedded systems share a recognizable set of characteristics:

They do one job, or a small set of jobs, defined when they are built. The user does not install new programs.
They are resource-constrained: limited memory, processing power and energy, and a low cost per unit.
They interact with the physical world through sensors and actuators, and often have real-time constraints.
They run unattended, often for years, and may be physically inaccessible after deployment.

Programmers moving from PCs to embedded systems should adjust a few habits:

Assume the device is unreachable. Over-the-air updates help (Chapter 16), but a device that crashes in a way that stops updates is lost.
Expect surprising inputs. A sensor can fail and report nonsense, or a network can deliver a burst of messages at once. Plan for it.
Log well. Faults may be impossible to reproduce on the bench, so error messages must carry enough context to diagnose them from a report.
Plan testing from the start. Testing on real hardware is slow; design the code so that most of it can be tested without the hardware.
Read datasheets. Embedded programmers write code that talks directly to hardware, and the datasheet is the specification.



Microprocessors, microcontrollers and SoCs
These terms are often confused because the categories overlap:

A microprocessor (MPU) is a CPU on a chip. It needs external memory and peripherals to be useful. Desktop and laptop processors are microprocessors.
A microcontroller (MCU) puts a CPU, memory (flash for the program, RAM for data) and peripherals on one chip: GPIO, ADC, timers, UART, SPI and I²C controllers, and an interrupt controller.
A system on a chip (SoC) integrates a microcontroller or microprocessor with larger subsystems, such as radios, graphics or cryptographic accelerators. The ESP32 is an SoC: a dual-core microcontroller plus Wi-Fi and Bluetooth radios.

Arm’s processor families show the same split. Cortex-M cores are designed for microcontrollers. Cortex-A cores are application processors with a memory-management unit that can run Linux (the Raspberry Pi uses them). Cortex-R cores target hard real-time, safety-critical systems such as disk controllers and automotive braking.


Real-time systems
A real-time system must respond to external events within a specified time. Being correct means producing the right answer on time; a right answer that arrives late is wrong.
Real-time systems are classified by what happens when a deadline is missed:

In a hard real-time system, a missed deadline is a failure. An airbag that deploys 200 ms late, or a motor controller that misses its control period, has failed.
In a soft real-time system, a missed deadline degrades quality but the system still works. A video stream drops a frame; a thermostat updates its display a little late.

“Real-time” does not mean “fast”. The deadline might be microseconds or hours. What matters is that the response time is bounded and predictable (deterministic).


Architecture 1: the super-loop
The simplest structure is the one you have used so far: a single infinite loop that does each job in turn.
void loop() {
  readSensors();
  updateControl();
  refreshDisplay();
  sendTelemetry();
}
Super-loops are small, easy to understand and need no operating system. Their weakness is that every job waits for every other job. If sendTelemetry() blocks for two seconds waiting for Wi-Fi, the control loop stops for two seconds too. Running jobs at different rates also gets awkward quickly, as you saw with the millis() scheduler in Chapter 3.


Architecture 2: foreground/background
A common improvement adds interrupts. The super-loop runs in the background, while interrupt service routines handle urgent events in the foreground. Most low-cost microcontroller products are built this way.
The weakness moves rather than disappears. Urgent work must either be done inside the ISR, making the ISR long and delaying other interrupts, or deferred to the background loop, where it waits until the loop gets around to it.


Architecture 3: a real-time kernel
A real-time kernel divides the program into independent tasks (also called threads), each with its own stack and a priority. A scheduler decides which task runs at any moment. In a preemptive kernel, a higher-priority task that becomes ready immediately interrupts a lower-priority one.
The kernel also provides tools for tasks to cooperate safely:

Delays that put a task to sleep without wasting processor time;
Queues for passing messages between tasks, and from ISRs to tasks;
Semaphores and mutexes to signal events and protect shared resources, such as an I²C bus;
Software timers that run a function at a set interval.

A real-time operating system (RTOS) is a real-time kernel plus higher-level services: file systems, network stacks, device drivers and so on.
The kernel costs some memory and processor time. In return, you get more predictable timing, a program split into smaller and more manageable pieces, and priorities that can be changed in software.


FreeRTOS on the ESP32
Many small RTOSes exist: FreeRTOS, Zephyr, RIOT, Apache Mynewt, Azure RTOS ThreadX (now Eclipse ThreadX), and commercial systems such as VxWorks. FreeRTOS is the most widely used. It is MIT-licensed, has been ported to dozens of processor families, and has been maintained by Amazon since 2017.
The ESP32’s software is built on FreeRTOS. Even a plain Arduino sketch runs on it: the framework starts a task called loopTask that calls loop() repeatedly, while Wi-Fi and Bluetooth run in their own tasks, mostly on the other core. You can create your own tasks alongside it:
#include &lt;Arduino.h&gt;
#include "iot_pins.h"

void blinkTask(void* param) {
  uint8_t pin = (uint8_t)(uintptr_t)param;
  uint32_t periodMs = (pin == IoTPins::LED_1) ? 1000 : 1500;
  pinMode(pin, OUTPUT);
  for (;;) {                                   // a task never returns
    digitalWrite(pin, !digitalRead(pin));
    vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(periodMs));       // sleep; other tasks run
  }
}

void setup() {
  xTaskCreatePinnedToCore(blinkTask, "red",    2048, (void*)(uintptr_t)IoTPins::LED_1, 1, nullptr, 1);
  xTaskCreatePinnedToCore(blinkTask, "yellow", 2048, (void*)(uintptr_t)IoTPins::LED_2, 1, nullptr, 1);
}

void loop() {
  vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(1000));             // loop() is just another task
}
The arguments to xTaskCreatePinnedToCore() are the task function, a name, the stack size in bytes, a parameter, the priority, an optional handle, and the core to run on.
Queues are the safe way to pass data between tasks, or from an ISR to a task:
QueueHandle_t buttonEvents = xQueueCreate(8, sizeof(uint32_t));

void IRAM_ATTR onButton() {
  uint32_t t = millis();
  BaseType_t woken = pdFALSE;
  xQueueSendFromISR(buttonEvents, &t, &woken);   // the ISR only posts an event
  portYIELD_FROM_ISR(woken);
}

void handlerTask(void*) {
  uint32_t t;
  for (;;) {
    if (xQueueReceive(buttonEvents, &t, portMAX_DELAY)) {  // sleeps until an event arrives
      Serial.printf("Button at %lu ms\n", t);              // safe to print here
    }
  }
}
This pattern keeps ISRs tiny, as Chapter 3 recommended, while guaranteeing that every event is processed promptly by a task whose priority you control.


Do you need an RTOS?
Not always. Many IoT devices are naturally event-driven: “sleep until something happens, handle it, go back to sleep”. A foreground/background design with deep sleep may be simpler and use less energy. Some designs use only part of a kernel, such as a scheduler and queues.
An RTOS earns its place when a device has several activities with different timing needs, such as a control loop, a user interface and a network connection, and when one slow activity must not stall the others.






TipTry it on your kit: one loop versus three tasks




Using a super-loop with delay(), try to blink LED 1 every 1 s, LED 2 every 2 s and LED 3 every 1.5 s. Why is this awkward?
Implement the same behavior with three FreeRTOS tasks. How many lines does each version take?
Add a fourth task that simulates a slow network call with vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(3000)). Does the blinking change? What would happen in the super-loop version?
Replace the button-counter ISR from Chapter 3 with the queue pattern above.

7.2 Concepts

What an actuator is
An actuator converts a control signal, usually a small electrical signal or a digital command, plus a source of energy into a physical effect: motion, force, light, sound, heat or an image. Actuators are the natural complement to sensors. Many devices contain both: a smart thermostat senses temperature and drives a relay that turns the furnace on.


The Observe–Decide–Act loop
IoT actuation follows a simple cycle, the Observe–Decide–Act (ODA) loop described by Rayes and Salam (Rayes and Salam 2022) (Figure 7.1):






Figure 7.1: The Observe–Decide–Act loop. The physical effect of each action changes what the device observes next.




Observe: read sensors, or receive data from other devices.
Decide: compare the observations with a goal (a set point) and choose an action. The decision can happen on the device, at a nearby gateway or in the cloud.
Act: drive the actuators. The action changes the physical world, which changes the next observation.

When the decision uses the result of earlier actions, the loop is a closed-loop (feedback) control system. A thermostat is closed-loop: it measures the temperature its furnace has produced. A lawn sprinkler on a timer is open-loop: it waters on schedule whether or not the soil is already wet.
Where the decision is made matters. A loop closed on the device can respond in milliseconds and keeps working when the network fails. A loop closed in the cloud can use more data and more computation, but inherits the network’s delay and failures. Chapter 18 returns to this choice.


Classifying actuators







Dimension
Options




Type of motion
None (static), linear, rotary; one, two or three axes


Output
Binary (on/off, open/closed) or continuous (position, speed, brightness)


Power
Micro-power (an LED), low power (a hobby servo), high power (an industrial motor)


Energy source
Electrical, hydraulic, pneumatic, thermal, manual


Application
Consumer, automotive, healthcare, industrial, agricultural



Some binary actuators are bistable: they hold their last state without power. A latching relay or a latching solenoid valve stays where it was put, which saves energy in battery-powered devices but means a power failure leaves them in an unknown state.


Families of actuators

Electric motors convert electrical energy into rotation. DC motors spin continuously, with speed set by voltage or PWM. Servo motors add a gearbox, a position sensor and a controller so they move to a commanded angle. Stepper motors move in precise steps and hold their position without feedback.
Linear actuators convert rotation into straight-line motion, typically with a lead screw.
Solenoids are electromagnets that pull a metal plunger. They drive door locks and valves.
Relays are switches operated by an electromagnet (or by a semiconductor, in solid-state relays). They let a low-power circuit switch a high-power one, such as a mains-powered heater.
Hydraulic actuators use pressurized liquid. By Pascal’s law, a pressure applied to a confined liquid is transmitted throughout it, so a small piston can drive a large one with great force. They power excavators and aircraft control surfaces.
Pneumatic actuators use compressed air in the same way, trading force for speed and cleanliness. They are common in factory automation.
Non-mechanical actuators produce information rather than motion: lights, displays and speakers.



Non-mechanical actuators

Lights
LEDs are the most common actuator of all. They can be binary (a status light) or continuous (dimmed with PWM, Chapter 3). A traffic light is a familiar example of a binary, stable-state output, and is the subject of Lab 2.


Displays
A display converts electrical signals into an image. Common technologies are LCD (a backlight shining through liquid crystals), OLED (each pixel emits its own light, so black pixels use no power), LED matrices, and e-paper (which holds an image with no power at all, ideal for labels and signs that change rarely).
The kit’s display is a 128 × 64-pixel monochrome OLED driven by an SSD1306 controller over SPI (Chapter 5). Like most small displays, it keeps a frame buffer in the microcontroller’s RAM: your code draws into the buffer, then sends the whole buffer to the display in one transfer.
#include &lt;SPI.h&gt;
#include &lt;Adafruit_GFX.h&gt;
#include &lt;Adafruit_SSD1306.h&gt;
#include "iot_pins.h"

Adafruit_SSD1306 oled(128, 64, &SPI, IoTPins::OLED_DC, IoTPins::OLED_RES, IoTPins::OLED_CS);

void setup() {
  oled.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC);
  oled.clearDisplay();
  oled.setTextSize(2);
  oled.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  oled.setCursor(0, 0);
  oled.println("Hello,");
  oled.println("IoT!");
  oled.display();                 // nothing appears until the buffer is sent
}

void loop() {}


Sound
A speaker moves a diaphragm with a coil in a magnetic field; it needs an amplifier to be driven from a microcontroller. A piezo buzzer is simpler: a ceramic disc that flexes when a voltage is applied. A passive piezo, like the kit’s, makes a tone at whatever frequency you drive it with; an active buzzer contains its own oscillator and only beeps at one pitch.
tone(IoTPins::BUZZER, 880, 200);   // 880 Hz for 200 ms (uses an LEDC channel)



Driving loads safely
A microcontroller pin can supply only a few milliamperes at 3.3 V. Most actuators need more current, a higher voltage, or both. Connecting them directly damages the pin or resets the board. The standard techniques are:

Use a transistor or MOSFET as a switch. The GPIO pin drives the transistor’s input; the transistor switches the load’s current from a separate supply. Logic-level MOSFETs are the usual choice for DC loads.
Use a separate supply for motors, and join the grounds. The servo on the kit runs from its own 5 V rail fed from the board’s VIN pin, which carries USB power before the 3.3 V regulator. The servo’s signal wire still comes from a GPIO pin, so the two circuits must share ground.
Add a flyback diode across inductive loads. Motors, relays and solenoids are coils. When the current through a coil is switched off, it produces a voltage spike that can destroy the transistor. A diode across the coil gives that energy a safe path.
Add a bulk capacitor near motors. A servo draws large current pulses when it starts moving, which can make the supply voltage dip and reset the ESP32. The kit puts a 470 µF capacitor across the servo rail to supply those pulses locally.
Never connect the 5 V servo rail to the 3.3 V rail. The ESP32’s pins are not 5 V tolerant.



Controlling a servo
A hobby servo reads a pulse every 20 ms (50 Hz). The width of the pulse sets the angle: typically about 1.0 ms for one end of the travel, 1.5 ms for the center and 2.0 ms for the other end, although many servos accept a wider range of roughly 0.5 to 2.5 ms. The ESP32’s LEDC peripheral generates these pulses:
// Arduino-ESP32 core 2.x API
constexpr int SERVO_CH = 2;           // channel 0 is used by tone()
constexpr int SERVO_HZ = 50;          // one pulse every 20 ms
constexpr int SERVO_BITS = 16;        // 65 536 steps per 20 ms period

void servoWriteUs(uint32_t us) {
  uint32_t duty = (uint32_t)((uint64_t)us * (1u &lt;&lt; SERVO_BITS) / 20000u);
  ledcWrite(SERVO_CH, duty);
}

void servoWriteDeg(float deg) {
  deg = constrain(deg, 0.0f, 180.0f);
  servoWriteUs(500 + (uint32_t)(deg / 180.0f * 2000.0f));   // 0.5–2.5 ms
}

void setup() {
  ledcSetup(SERVO_CH, SERVO_HZ, SERVO_BITS);
  ledcAttachPin(IoTPins::SERVO, SERVO_CH);
  servoWriteDeg(90);                  // start at the center
}

void loop() {}
Libraries such as ESP32Servo wrap this, but writing it once shows that a servo command is nothing more than a carefully timed PWM signal.


Deciding: simple control strategies
The “decide” step can be as simple as a threshold or as complex as a machine-learning model. Three strategies cover most IoT devices:

On/off control switches the actuator fully on or off around a set point. Without care it chatters: a heater near its set point switches on and off many times a second. Hysteresis fixes this by using two thresholds: turn the heater on below 20.5 °C and off above 21.5 °C.
void controlHeater(float tempC) {
  static bool heaterOn = false;
  if (!heaterOn && tempC &lt; 20.5f) heaterOn = true;
  if ( heaterOn && tempC &gt; 21.5f) heaterOn = false;
  digitalWrite(HEATER_PIN, heaterOn ? HIGH : LOW);
}
Proportional control sets the output in proportion to the error, the difference between the set point and the measurement: \(u = K_p\,(r - y)\). It responds smoothly, but alone it usually settles slightly off target.
PID control adds an integral term, which removes that steady error, and a derivative term, which damps overshoot. PID controllers run inside most motors, drones and industrial processes.

Every control loop must also run fast enough: if the decision step is slower than the physical process it controls, the loop becomes sluggish or unstable.


Fail-safe design
Before writing control code, decide what each actuator should do when something goes wrong: the sensor fails, the network drops, the firmware crashes or the power fails. A fail-safe state is one that causes the least harm. A heater should fail off; a door lock on a fire exit should fail unlocked; a pump protecting a basement from flooding might need to fail on. Watchdog timers, sensor plausibility checks and command timeouts are how software enforces those choices.






TipTry it on your kit: an Observe–Decide–Act loop



This exercise corresponds to the Observe–Decide–Act lecture activity and uses Stage 3 of the kit build (the servo rail).

Observe: read the potentiometer and smooth it with an exponential moving average.
Decide: map the reading to a servo angle between 0° and 180°.
Act: move the servo, and show the angle on the OLED.
Add a rule: if the angle exceeds 150°, sound the buzzer and light LED 1. Add hysteresis so the alarm does not chatter near the threshold.
Unplug the potentiometer’s wiper wire (with the USB cable disconnected first, then reconnect power). What does the servo do? Design a plausibility check and a fail-safe position for this case.

A guided, step-by-step version for a lecture session is Activity 4.

A starting structure
#include &lt;Arduino.h&gt;
#include "iot_pins.h"

enum class Light { RED, RED_YELLOW, GREEN, YELLOW };

constexpr uint32_t RED_MS = 10000, AMBER_MS = 2000, MIN_GREEN_AFTER_PRESS_MS = 5000;

Light state = Light::RED;            // what is the safest initial state?
uint32_t stateStart = 0;
bool requested = false;              // pedestrian pressed the button
uint32_t requestTime = 0;

void setLights(bool r, bool y, bool g) {
