uint8_t read8(uint8_t reg) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(0x76);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.endTransmission(false);           // repeated START
  Wire.requestFrom((uint8_t)0x76, (uint8_t)1);
  return Wire.read();
}

uint16_t read16LE(uint8_t reg) {          // calibration words are little-endian
  Wire.beginTransmission(0x76);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.endTransmission(false);
  Wire.requestFrom((uint8_t)0x76, (uint8_t)2);
  uint16_t lo = Wire.read(), hi = Wire.read();
  return (hi &lt;&lt; 8) | lo;
}

Part 1: detecting steps
When you walk with the kit in your hand or pocket, each step produces a peak in the magnitude of the acceleration:
\[
|a| = \sqrt{a_x^2 + a_y^2 + a_z^2}
\]
Using the magnitude makes the detector independent of how the kit is oriented. At rest, \(|a| \approx 1\) g.

Configure the MPU-6050 for a ±4 g range (register 0x1C, ACCEL_CONFIG), where the sensitivity is 8 192 counts per g. Sample at a steady rate, such as 50 Hz, using millis().
Smooth the magnitude with a short moving average or an exponential moving average (6  Sensors).
Detect peaks: count a step when the smoothed magnitude rises above an upper threshold and then falls below a lower threshold (hysteresis), and ignore new peaks for a short refractory period (around 250–300 ms) after each step, since people do not take more than three or four steps per second.
Show the step count on the OLED.

// Sketch of a peak detector with hysteresis and a refractory period
constexpr float HIGH_G = 1.20f, LOW_G = 1.05f;      // starting values: tune them
constexpr uint32_t REFRACTORY_MS = 300;

uint32_t steps = 0;

void onSample(float magSmoothed) {
  static bool above = false;
  static uint32_t lastStep = 0;
  uint32_t now = millis();
  if (!above && magSmoothed &gt; HIGH_G && now - lastStep &gt; REFRACTORY_MS) {
    above = true;
    steps++;
    lastStep = now;
  } else if (above && magSmoothed &lt; LOW_G) {
    above = false;
  }
}
The thresholds above are starting points. Tune them with data: print the smoothed magnitude over the serial port while walking, plot it, and choose thresholds that separate steps from noise.

